R version 3.6.3 (2020-02-29); checkmate 2.3.4

A publicly constructed atom can retain repeated matrix elements:
structure(c("b", "b"), .Dim = 1:2)
Its negation is a flat unique complement:
c("a", "c")
The ordinary Clause constructor globally deduplicates and flattens:
list(X = "b")
Formula negation constructs the same canonical complement directly:
list(list(X = c("a", "c")))

Double atom negation preserves support, but uses stored domain order:
structure(c("b", "a", "b", "a"), .Dim = c(2L, 2L))
c("a", "b")

Six ordinary scalar symbol-name representations produced the same canonical name.

Previously recorded constructor exceptions, reproduced as exclusion controls:
clause_free_false_first: All symbols must be in the same universe.
clause_free_false_last: All symbols must be in the same universe.
formula_free_true_first: All clauses must be in the same universe.
nested_formula_then_false: wrong arguments for subsetting an environment
Previously recorded TRUE | proper_clause result class: logical
Previously recorded old-R mixed Ops warning: Incompatible methods ("&.CnfAtom", "&.CnfClause") for "&"
Old-R native mixed Ops error: operations are possible only for numeric, logical or complex types
Direct Formula method has correct truth on all nine valuations on both runtimes.
